From d5dc19ff990c040dc7de531a6b0a2350a009db68 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aadi Srivastava <aadisrivastava.iitm@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:46:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] Form interior pointers into array rows in loops that
 cannot reach the GC

The strength reduction in the previous commit turns four address recomputations
into four running indices.  Four fixed row addresses plus one shared index is
strictly better, and it is the shape a C port gets for free, but OCaml cannot
write it: the GC finds a block's header immediately before its data, so a
pointer into the middle of an array is not a representable value.

It is representable in a region the GC cannot see into.  Cmm already has the
type for it -- `Addr`, a derived heap pointer, produced today by bigarray
indexing -- and the amd64 emitter already enforces the rule, fatally, for every
frame descriptor: an `Addr` live where the collector could run is `bad GC root`.
So in a loop with no allocation, no call and no poll, a row address can be
formed once ahead of the loop instead of being rebuilt from a row index every
iteration.

The rewrite is re-association plus loop-invariant code motion of an address that
is already an `Addr`.  An access `a.(b + i)` reaches Cmm as

  (+a (+a a (<< (+ b i) 2)) -8)

with `i` an affine function `m * c + k` of the loop counter `c`.  Caddi, Cadda
and Clsl are wrapping native operations and shifting distributes over wrapping
addition, so on every input, overflow included, that is

  (+a (+a (+a a (<< b 2)) (<< c 3)) -4)

whose leading summand is loop-invariant.  Hoisting it leaves one addressing mode
`disp(base, counter, scale)` per row: four rows share one index register and one
increment, and the counter is used naked, so the retagging of the `for` counter
leaves the loop as well.  On the 4-way unrolled dot product the inner loop goes
from 18 instructions per four multiply-accumulates to 13.

Implemented on Cmm, extending the strength reduction, rather than on the CFG
after `Cfg_polling.instrument_fundecl`.  The CFG placement is the one that can
ask "is this loop poll-free" exactly, but the question has a degenerate answer:
`instr_cfg_with_layout` inserts a poll on a back edge unless the loop is already
`safe`, and `safe` means it contains a heap allocation -- which is itself a
safepoint and disqualifies the loop anyway.  So a user loop is poll-free exactly
when polling is disabled for the unit, which is a flag either analysis can read,
and the CFG's precision buys nothing while costing the loop structure: on Cmm a
loop is a `Ccatch Recursive` whose parameters are the loop-carried values, and
hoisting is wrapping the node in a `Clet`.

Interior pointers run before the strength reduction and win where both could
fire: the sum `b + i` the strength reduction looks for is gone from the
addresses this rewrote, so a row cannot end up with both a hoisted base and a
running index.  Where no invariant term can be hoisted the pass declines and
leaves the loop to the strength reduction unchanged.

Conditions.  The whole `Ccatch` must be free of operations at which a frame
descriptor may be emitted or the GC may run; the list is a complete match over
Cmm's operations, so a new one is a compile error here rather than a silently
permitted safepoint.  `Craise` is not one of them -- the emitter records a
raise's frame with an empty live set, and `Raise_notrace` records no frame at
all -- so a checked array access keeps its bound checks, consuming the same
index expression, and still transforms.  Polls do not exist yet at Cmm time, so
their absence is established from `Config.poll_insertion`,
`-disable-poll-insertion` and `[@poll error]` rather than observed; deciding it
here keeps the failure mode "the pass declines" rather than the backend's
`bad GC root`, which names neither a loop nor a source location.

Off with -no-cmm-interior-pointers.
